# Backend Architecture: Layered + DDD

Reference: `core/docs/architecture/backend-overview.md`, `core/docs/architecture/backend-layers.md`

## Dependencies

`Presentation → Application → Domain`; Infrastructure implements Application/Domain ports. Dependencies point inward only.

- Controllers validate DTOs, call a use case/service, and return responses. No business rules or direct DB/Redis/ORM access.
- A use case has one public `execute()` and orchestrates domain, repositories, and cache. Move limits, statuses, and calculations to domain behavior.
- Entities own invariants and state transitions through methods such as `publish()`; external code must not mutate state directly.
- Domain services contain pure cross-entity rules with no NestJS, TypeORM, Redis, or HTTP dependencies.
- Infrastructure owns TypeORM entities/repositories, query building, external adapters, and cache implementations.
- Keep methods focused; split orchestration before a method exceeds 50 lines.

## Boundaries

- Bounded contexts: Listings, Auth, Users, Catalog, Favorites, SavedFilters, Admin, Upload, Geography.
- A module owns and registers only its entities; communicate across modules through public services/ports.

## Target module structure

```
module/
├── presentation/    (controller, dto/)
├── application/     (use-cases/, ports/)
├── domain/          (entities/, value-objects/, services/, types.ts)
├── infrastructure/  (persistence/, cache/)
└── module.ts
```

New code follows this structure; migrate legacy code incrementally.
